#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176723} :activity/id #uuid "55f43435-8dca-5810-9753-eee340bb8532" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048485 :topic/id #uuid "a385b92b-792f-5dd9-9921-9b85efbacd4d" :topic/math-academy-id 3603} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176723 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176991 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we might know the general form of the probability distribution for a population, yet we may not know some of its underlying parameters. We wish to develop strategies to estimate unknown population parameters from sample data. One such strategy is to use **likelihood functions.**\n\nIn this lesson, we'll learn how to compute likelihood functions for particular discrete probability distributions.\n\nSuppose we have an I.I.D. (independent and identically distributed) random sample\n\n$$\nX_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_n\n$$\n\nwhere the probability mass function of each $X_i$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nand $\\theta$ is an *unknown,* fixed parameter.\n\nSuppose we have a particular sample from this distribution:\n\n$$\nx_1, \\: x_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: x_n\n$$\n\nWe define the **likelihood function** $L(\\theta)$ as\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n)\n$$\n\nThe likelihood function gives the probability of getting a particular sample for a specific parameter value.\n\nNow, since we assumed that all $X_i$'s are independent and identically distributed, by the multiplication law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= P(X_{1} = x_{1}, X_{2} = x_{2}, …, X_{n} = x_{n}) \\\\\n&= P(X_{1} = x_{1}) \\cdot P(X_{2} = x_{2})⋯P(X_{n} = x_{n}) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)P(X_{i} = x_{i}) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo summarize, the likelihood function $L(\\theta)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nLet's take a look at a concrete example." :tutorial/id #uuid "febfe0fc-b4fd-5ec4-9fe2-66459c06433b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "05cbc6df-2e50-58cc-b377-41b56e25e5ed" :step/math-academy-id 33494 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176882}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176845 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175687 :question/id #uuid "d15d6003-a4dd-599f-987d-289508968ac7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10105"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39593326-76e2-578f-af68-57f59712c30e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049729} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051082}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176392}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "819b8cb4-51d8-5f27-b9c1-7715977c4527" :step/math-academy-id 38120 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176880}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176880 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176062 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175708 :question/id #uuid "3e3ca456-a46a-5a5b-9cf6-07caad631ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11117"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a2eb2ca-a19a-591e-810a-c028a17be252" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050651}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176393}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "808203c4-7f89-5eca-af00-29965de75553" :step/math-academy-id 42270 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176881}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176881 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176063 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175709 :question/id #uuid "9f08cf84-3e3b-5c86-adb6-622bc51a15f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11118"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03d91774-9a9f-55a8-8a9a-090788318a98" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049358}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410435}] :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "44901d9a-fe9f-55ba-bd1c-9441ea7f0e71" :step/math-academy-id 42271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176882 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177074 :tutorial/content "Suppose that\n$X_1,\\quad X_2,\\quad X_3,\\quad X_4,\\quad X_5,\\quad X_6$\n\nis an I.I.D random sample from a population, where\n\n$X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$\nand $\\theta$ is the *unknown* probability of success.\n\nSuppose we conduct a sample and get the following data:\n\n$$\nx_1=0, \\quad x_2=1, \\quad x_3=1, \\quad x_4=0, \\quad x_5=1, \\quad x_6=1\n$$\n\nLet's compute the likelihood function for this sample.\n\nRecall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are I.I.D random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta)$, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is given by\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots, X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta)\n$$\n\nThe probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable with an unknown probability of success $\\theta$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0,1\n$$\n\nUsing this definition, we can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)f(x_{i} \\\\\n&= θ) \\\\\n&= ∏_(i = 1)^(n)θ^{x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{i}} \\\\\n&= (θ^{x_{1}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{1}}) \\cdot (θ^{x_{2}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{2}})⋯(θ^{x_{n}}(1 - θ)^{1 - x_{n}}) \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{∑(1 - x_{i})} \\\\\n&= θ^{∑x_{i}}(1 - θ)^{n - ∑x_{i}} \\\\\n&= θ^{S}(1 - θ)^{n - S}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i\n$$\n\nFor our sample, we have that $n=6$, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= 0 + 1 + 1 + 0 + 1 + 1 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the likelihood function for this sample is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(θ) &= θ^{4}(1 - θ)^{6 - 4} \\\\\n&= θ^{4}(1 - θ)^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph of $L(\\theta)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/28/28c73749a566d5531ef2f13c5b01bd1d26ced28e19749abf056841492fb497f5.png)\n\nNotice that $L(\\theta)$ has a maximum inside the interval $\\theta \\in [0,1]$. The value $\\widehat{\\,\\theta\\,}$ that corresponds to the maximum likelihood is called the **maximum likelihood estimate** of the parameter $\\theta$ for the given sample.\n\nWe'll discuss how to compute the maximum likelihood estimate in future topics. But, for now, let's see more examples of finding the likelihood functions for different distributions and samples." :tutorial/id #uuid "8748c53f-183a-5ba8-99aa-e0a92e7d9707" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7013 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example"} :step/id #uuid "7abb8790-00de-50e4-8248-7dcfdef20be2" :step/math-academy-id 42315 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176845}}] :activity/title "Likelihood Functions for Discrete Probability Distributions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176143}]}
